cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  yamanaka-replay: adaptive experimental design recovers the Yamanaka factors
  in an order of magnitude fewer experiments, and fails silently when the
  recipe size is assumed
message: If you use this software or its results, please cite it.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Mashal
    family-names: Alkhalifah
    name-particle: Saleh
    affiliation: >-
      Institute of Smart Cities, King Abdulaziz City for Science and
      Technology (KACST), Riyadh, Saudi Arabia
    email: mkhalifah@kacst.gov.sa
repository-code: https://github.com/OWNER/yamanaka-replay
abstract: >-
  An in-silico replay of the 2006 discovery of the Yamanaka reprogramming
  factors under its original fitness function -- the colony count surviving
  Fbx15/G418 selection -- measuring cost in wet-lab experiments rather than
  compute. Over 300 paired replicates, adaptive Bayesian experimental design
  recovers the four-factor recipe in a median of 6-10 experiments against 61
  for the historical leave-one-out protocol, and is cheaper in 300 of 300
  runs. An ablation shows the saving comes from informative experiment
  selection rather than from Bayesian inference alone. A campaign of 6,320
  replicates then isolates the method's limits. Absence of graded synergy is
  found to be a calibration error rather than a structural one: with no
  partial signal at all, a correctly specified searcher still succeeds in
  100 percent of runs. The one structural failure is fixed recipe
  cardinality, which fails silently and symmetrically -- 0 percent success at
  0.999 reported confidence for both three- and five-factor truths -- and is
  removed here by treating recipe size as a latent variable, restoring 100
  percent success for a premium of one experiment. A recurring pattern is
  reported across three independent designs: more efficient experiment
  selection makes model error less detectable. The oracle is a simulator
  calibrated to published figures; no biological claim is made.
keywords:
  - adaptive experimental design
  - Bayesian optimal experimental design
  - combinatorial search
  - cellular reprogramming
  - induced pluripotent stem cells
  - group testing
  - latent cardinality
  - model misspecification
